# .NET / C# Development Rules

## Nullable Reference Types
- Enable nullable reference types in all projects (`<Nullable>enable</Nullable>`)
- Use `string?` for nullable strings, `string` for non-nullable
- Handle null checks explicitly — don't suppress warnings with `!` unless justified
- Use null-conditional operators (`?.`) and null-coalescing (`??`) appropriately

## Async/Await
- Use `async`/`await` for all I/O-bound operations
- Suffix async methods with `Async` (e.g., `GetUserAsync`)
- Never use `.Result` or `.Wait()` on tasks — this causes deadlocks
- Use `ConfigureAwait(false)` in library code
- Return `Task` not `void` from async methods (except event handlers)
- Use `CancellationToken` for cancellable operations
- Pass `CancellationToken` through the entire call chain

## Dependency Injection
- Register services in `Program.cs` or dedicated extension methods
- Use constructor injection — don't use the service locator pattern
- Prefer `IServiceCollection` extension methods for registration
- Use appropriate lifetimes: Singleton, Scoped, Transient
- Don't inject `IServiceProvider` directly — inject specific dependencies

## Entity Framework (if applicable)
- Use migrations for all schema changes
- Use `AsNoTracking()` for read-only queries
- Avoid loading entire entities when you only need specific fields — use `.Select()`
- Use parameterized queries — never string interpolation in raw SQL
- Handle concurrency with optimistic concurrency tokens
- Use `IDbContextFactory` for background services

## Naming Conventions
- **PascalCase**: Classes, methods, properties, events, namespaces
- **camelCase**: Local variables, parameters
- **_camelCase**: Private fields (with underscore prefix)
- **IPascalCase**: Interfaces (prefix with I)
- **PascalCaseAsync**: Async methods (suffix with Async)

## LINQ
- Prefer method syntax for simple queries, query syntax for complex joins
- Use `FirstOrDefault` over `First` when null is a valid result
- Use `Any()` over `Count() > 0` for existence checks
- Be aware of deferred execution — materialize queries with `.ToList()` when needed
- Avoid multiple enumeration of `IEnumerable` — materialize first

## Error Handling
- Use specific exception types — don't throw `Exception` directly
- Create custom exception classes inheriting from appropriate base types
- Use `ExceptionFilter` or middleware for global exception handling in ASP.NET
- Don't catch exceptions you can't handle — let them propagate
- Log exceptions with structured logging (Serilog, Microsoft.Extensions.Logging)
- Include correlation IDs in error responses

## Security
- Use `[Authorize]` attributes on controllers/endpoints
- Validate all model inputs with data annotations or FluentValidation
- Use `IDataProtectionProvider` for encryption
- Use `HTTPS` redirection middleware
- Configure CORS explicitly — don't use `AllowAll` in production
- Use anti-forgery tokens for form submissions
- Sanitize user input before rendering in views

## Logging & Observability
- Use `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging` with `ILogger<T>`
- Configure structured logging with Serilog or similar
- Use log scopes for correlation: `using (_logger.BeginScope(new { CorrelationId = id }))`
- Log levels:
  - Debug: Detailed diagnostic (dev only)
  - Information: Normal operations (request start/end, service calls)
  - Warning: Recoverable issues (deprecated usage, retry)
  - Error: Failures (unhandled exceptions, service failures)
- Use dot-separated UpperCamelCase for log category names
- Add health checks: `app.MapHealthChecks("/health")`
- Never log: passwords, tokens, connection strings, PII

## Performance
- Use `IMemoryCache` or `IDistributedCache` for caching
- Use `ResponseCaching` middleware where appropriate
- Profile with BenchmarkDotNet for performance-critical code
- Use `Span<T>` and `Memory<T>` for high-performance buffer operations
- Avoid boxing value types unnecessarily
- Use `IHttpClientFactory` to manage HttpClient lifetimes

## Testing
- Use xUnit or NUnit as the test framework
- Use `Moq` or `NSubstitute` for mocking
- Use `WebApplicationFactory<T>` for integration testing ASP.NET apps
- Write tests alongside implementation
- Test error paths and edge cases, not just happy paths

## Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Don't use `async void` (except for event handlers) — exceptions won't propagate
- Don't capture `HttpContext` in background tasks — it's disposed
- Don't mix `Task.Run` with async — it wastes thread pool threads
- Don't forget to dispose `HttpClient` — use `IHttpClientFactory`
- Don't use `DateTime.Now` — use `DateTime.UtcNow` or `DateTimeOffset`
- Don't suppress nullable warnings with `!` without documenting why
